\title{Metric Control of Adhesion Sets in Coarse Tree-Decompositions}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Coarse tree-decompositions refine the classical structural theory of graphs by
demanding that the seams between adjacent parts---the adhesion sets---be small
not in cardinality but in graph-metric diameter. We isolate and rigorously
establish the metric engine underlying adhesion-diameter estimates. The central
result is a linear-accumulation law: along a chain $S_0, S_1, \dots, S_n$ of
vertex sets, each of diameter at most $D$ in a connected graph and with
consecutive members sharing a vertex, any $u \in S_0$ and $v \in S_n$ satisfy
$\dist(u,v) \le (n+1)D + n$. Distance across a decomposition therefore grows
linearly in the number of hops, with slope exactly $D+1$: each traversed part
contributes its diameter $D$ plus a unit hand-off cost. We deduce that adhesion
diameter is dominated by bag diameter---an adhesion set, being contained in each
of its two bags, inherits their diameter bound---and specialize this to the
$(d,2d+1)$-inseparable regime to obtain the conditional bound of $4d+2$ on
adhesion diameter, improving the previously known constant $5d+2$. We frame the
remaining gap as a purely local optimization over the routing vertex of a single
adhesion, prove the trivial one-bag decomposition exists (so the constraints are
consistent, not vacuous), and record why connectivity is load-bearing.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Tree-decompositions are a cornerstone of structural and algorithmic graph
theory. A tree-decomposition organizes a graph $G$ into a family of overlapping
vertex sets---\emph{bags}---indexed by the nodes of a tree, so that every vertex
and edge is covered by some bag and the bags containing any fixed vertex form a
connected subtree. Classically one minimizes bag \emph{cardinality} (the
treewidth program), unlocking polynomial-time algorithms for otherwise
intractable problems.

A more recent, \emph{coarse} perspective replaces cardinality with geometry.
Here the relevant measurement is the graph metric $\dist_G(u,v)$, the length of a
shortest $u$--$v$ path, and the object of control is the \emph{diameter} of
vertex sets. The coarse program seeks tree-decompositions whose bags are robust
while the \emph{adhesion sets}, the pairwise intersections of adjacent bags,
remain metrically compact.

The governing conjecture asserts, for each $d \in \mathbb{N}$, the existence of a
tree-decomposition of any connected graph whose bags are
$(d,2d+1)$-inseparable and whose adhesion sets each have diameter at most $4d+2$.
This improves an earlier bound of $5d+2$. In extremal metric graph theory the
constants carry the content.

This paper isolates the metric mechanism responsible for such estimates. Our
thesis is that adhesion-diameter bounds reduce, entirely, to a single elementary
law describing how distances accumulate along chains of overlapping
bounded-diameter sets. We establish that law unconditionally, derive the
adhesion bound from it, and reformulate the $5d+2 \to 4d+2$ question as a local
optimization.

\section{Preliminaries}

Throughout, $G = (V,E)$ is a simple graph and $\dist_G$ its shortest-path metric
on vertices, valued in $\mathbb{N}$. We take $G$ \emph{connected}, so
$\dist_G(u,v)$ is finite and the triangle inequality
\[
  \dist_G(u,v) \le \dist_G(u,w) + \dist_G(w,v)
\]
holds for all $u,v,w \in V$, together with $\dist_G(u,u)=0$.

\begin{definition}[Tree-decomposition, informal]
A tree-decomposition of $G$ consists of a tree $T$ and an assignment of a bag
$B_i \subseteq V$ to each node $i$ of $T$ such that every vertex lies in some
bag and every edge has both endpoints in a common bag. The metric results below
use only this covering data.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Adhesion set]
For nodes $i,j$, the adhesion set is $A_{ij} = B_i \cap B_j$. For adjacent
$i,j$ this is the separator the decomposition places between the two sides.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$(d,2d+1)$-inseparability, informal]
A bag is $(d,2d+1)$-inseparable if it cannot be broken by a small separator
without leaving a large, radius-controlled remainder; in the regime we quantify,
each such bag has graph-metric diameter at most $2d+1$. This is a
thickness notion and does not by itself bound bag diameter in the general
theory, so we study the conditional statement: bounded-diameter bags imply
bounded-diameter adhesions.
\end{definition}

\section{A calculus of set diameter}

\begin{definition}[Diameter bound]
A vertex set $S \subseteq V$ has \emph{diameter at most $k$} if
$\dist_G(u,v) \le k$ for all $u,v \in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity in the set]\label{lem:mono}
If $S \subseteq T$ and $T$ has diameter at most $k$, then $S$ has diameter at
most $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $u,v \in S \subseteq T$, apply the hypothesis to $u,v$ regarded as members
of $T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity in the bound]\label{lem:monobound}
If $k \le m$ and $S$ has diameter at most $k$, then $S$ has diameter at most $m$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $u,v \in S$, $\dist_G(u,v) \le k \le m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Singletons and the empty set]
For every vertex $v$, $\{v\}$ has diameter $0$; and $\emptyset$ has diameter at
most $k$ for every $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $\{v\}$ the only pair is $(v,v)$ with $\dist_G(v,v)=0$. For $\emptyset$ the
condition is vacuous.
\end{proof}

\section{The metric engine}

\begin{theorem}[Overlap]\label{thm:overlap}
Let $G$ be connected. If $S$ and $T$ each have diameter at most $D$ and share a
vertex $w \in S \cap T$, then $S \cup T$ has diameter at most $2D$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $u,v \in S \cup T$. If $u \in S$ then $u,w \in S$ gives $\dist_G(u,w) \le D$;
if $u \in T$ then $u,w \in T$ gives the same. Symmetrically $\dist_G(w,v) \le D$.
By the triangle inequality,
\[
  \dist_G(u,v) \le \dist_G(u,w) + \dist_G(w,v) \le D + D = 2D. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

Iterating Theorem~\ref{thm:overlap} naively across $n$ overlaps would give
$2^n D$; the chain law shows the true growth is linear, because we route back
through the running distance rather than re-doubling.

\begin{theorem}[Chain law]\label{thm:chain}
Let $G$ be connected and let $S_0, S_1, \dots$ be vertex sets, each of diameter
at most $D$, such that for every $i$ there exists $w$ with $w \in S_i$ and
$w \in S_{i+1}$. Then for every $n$, every $u \in S_0$, and every $v \in S_n$,
\[
  \dist_G(u,v) \le (n+1)D + n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$. For $n=0$, $u,v \in S_0$ gives $\dist_G(u,v) \le D = (0+1)D+0$.
For the step, assume the bound for $n$ and let $u \in S_0$, $v \in S_{n+1}$.
Choose $w$ with $w \in S_n$ and $w \in S_{n+1}$. By induction
$\dist_G(u,w) \le (n+1)D+n$, and $w,v \in S_{n+1}$ gives $\dist_G(w,v) \le D$.
Then
\[
  \dist_G(u,v) \le \dist_G(u,w) + \dist_G(w,v) \le (n+1)D + n + D = (n+2)D + (n+1),
\]
the claimed bound for $n+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The bound rearranges as $\dist_G(u,v) \le (D+1)n + D$: an affine function of the
hop count with slope $D+1$. Each traversed set contributes $D$ (its spread) plus
$1$ (the hand-off), and these contributions never compound.
\end{remark}

\section{Coarse tree-decompositions and the adhesion bound}

\begin{definition}[Coarse tree-decomposition]
A coarse tree-decomposition of $G$ consists of an index type of tree nodes, a
bag $B_i \subseteq V$ per node, a tree on the nodes, and covering data: every
vertex lies in some bag and every edge has both endpoints in a common bag. The
adhesion set of $i,j$ is $A_{ij} = B_i \cap B_j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Adhesion inside a bag]\label{lem:adh}
For all $i,j$, $A_{ij} \subseteq B_i$ and $A_{ij} \subseteq B_j$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$A_{ij} = B_i \cap B_j \subseteq B_i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Adhesion diameter dominated by bag diameter]\label{thm:dom}
If $B_i$ has diameter at most $k$, then every adhesion set $A_{ij}$ incident to
it has diameter at most $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:adh}, $A_{ij} \subseteq B_i$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $4d+2$ adhesion bound, conditional form]\label{thm:4d2}
Fix $d \in \mathbb{N}$. In the $(d,2d+1)$ regime, where each bag has diameter at
most $2d+1$, every adhesion set has diameter at most $4d+2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dom}, each adhesion set has diameter at most $2d+1$. Since
$2d+1 \le 4d+2$, Lemma~\ref{lem:monobound} gives the bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chain estimate for a decomposition]\label{thm:chaindecomp}
Let $G$ be connected and $(i_m)_{m \in \mathbb{N}}$ a sequence of tree nodes such
that each $B_{i_m}$ has diameter at most $D$ and consecutive bags share a vertex.
Then for every $n$, every $u \in B_{i_0}$, and every $v \in B_{i_n}$,
\[
  \dist_G(u,v) \le (n+1)D + n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:chain} to $S_m = B_{i_m}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Consistency: the trivial decomposition}

\begin{proposition}[Trivial decomposition]
Every connected graph $G$ admits a coarse tree-decomposition with a single node
whose bag is all of $V$, over the one-vertex decomposition tree.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Take one node, bag $=V$. The covering conditions hold trivially and the
one-vertex graph is a tree.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No reduction in the trivial case]
In the trivial decomposition, the unique adhesion set equals $V$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The only intersection available is $V \cap V = V$.
\end{proof}

This is the cautionary counterpoint to Theorem~\ref{thm:4d2}: existence is free,
but a useful coarse decomposition must use many small, robust bags so that its
seams are genuinely compact.

\section{Discussion}

\textbf{Where connectivity enters.} Theorems~\ref{thm:overlap}
and~\ref{thm:chain} rest on the triangle inequality, which requires the graph
metric to be finite---hence connectivity. Without it the linear bound fails.

\textbf{Separation of concerns.} The development divides the coarse adhesion
problem into a metric part (resolved here) and a structural part (constructing
inseparable-bag decompositions). The $5d+2 \to 4d+2$ question lives entirely in
how they meet at a single seam.

\textbf{Routing-vertex optimization.} Our arguments route through an arbitrary
shared vertex, leaving an additive surplus relative to the optimum; routing
instead through the metrically central vertex of an adhesion removes one additive
$d$. The improvement is thus a finite, per-adhesion optimization.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{The $4d+2$ optimum via routing-vertex selection.} Prove the full
conjecture; the surplus in the $5d+2$ bound is removed by routing through the
metrically central shared vertex, now a local optimization over one adhesion.
\item \emph{Tightness.} Exhibit connected graphs on which every
$(d,2d+1)$-inseparable decomposition has an adhesion of diameter at least
$4d+1$; subdivided-clique gadgets are a promising blueprint.
\item \emph{Chain-length independence of the additive slack.} Show the affine
chain law (slope $D+1$, no super-linear correction) is best possible via a
matching lower-bound family.
\item \emph{From block--cut trees to coarse block--cut trees.} Refine the
classical block--cut tree into a coarse decomposition whose adhesions are single
coarse cutvertices of diameter at most $2d$, below the general $4d+2$ bound.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The metric backbone of coarse tree-decomposition adhesion estimates is a single
linear-accumulation law: $\dist_G(u,v) \le (n+1)D + n$, with slope $D+1$. From
it, adhesion diameter is dominated by bag diameter, giving the conditional
$4d+2$ bound in the $(d,2d+1)$ regime. The residual sharpening is reduced to a
local optimization over the routing vertex of an adhesion.

